<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    THE BROWSER TEST LEVEL - the sixth test project, and the resolution of blocker B5.

    Why it exists at all: two AD-29 invariants live only in the rendered client and are
    unreachable from every suite the Structural Seed names. ArchUnitNET reads compiled IL,
    the slice suite runs in-process, and neither can observe which Task holds focus after a
    Board pages. test-design-architecture.md:315 records that as testability concern T9 and
    asks for "the browser-test binding decision, and a sixth test project". This is both.

    The immediate reason it exists NOW rather than at story 2.8: story 1.3 shipped the first
    rendered surface, closed with 136 passing tests, and the client did not start in any
    browser in any locale. Every one of those 136 tests reads a FILE - markup is parsed, copy
    is scanned, tokens are checked for contrast, compiled IL is read for the document-language
    call. Nothing loaded the page. A composition root that throws before the first render
    passed all of them, which is not a gap at the margin of what the suite claims: it is the
    difference between an application that works and one that cannot start.

    A DECLARED ADDITION TO THE STRUCTURAL SEED, not a variance. Yello.Tests.Shared is the
    variance - infrastructure holding no cases. This project holds cases and is a suite, so
    AC5's "later stories add cases to existing suites rather than creating suites" is the rule
    it has to answer to. It answers by being the conversation TESTING-CONVENTIONS.md requires:
    "If a new suite seems necessary, that is a conversation about the architecture, not a
    project template." Held with Lee on 2026-09-07, on the record in the TF run's progress
    document, and the alternative considered was folding these cases into Yello.Tests.Slices.
    They were kept apart because this suite's entry criterion is a live dev-server process and
    a real browser, which nothing else here needs.

    NO CONTAINER, AND THAT IS A PROPERTY OF THE CLIENT RATHER THAN A CHOICE. Yello.Client is a
    standalone WebAssembly application with its own dev server; Yello.Host serves it nothing.
    So this suite starts one process, needs no SQL Server, and belongs in the PR tier despite
    being a browser suite. Cases that need a signed-in Session or real data arrive with the
    stories that build them, and those will need more.
  -->

  <!--
    Runs on Microsoft.Testing.Platform only: xunit.v3 4.0.0 depends on
    xunit.v3.mtp-v2 [4.0.0]. There is no VSTest path here, so Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk is
    deliberately absent.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <IsTestProject>true</IsTestProject>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    NO `ignore-exit-code 8` SWITCH. This suite ships with cases, so the switch would be a lie
    in the one direction that matters: with it in place a trait typo or broken discovery makes
    the suite report success having run nothing.

    `Only_suites_with_no_tests_may_ignore_the_zero_test_exit_code` in the architecture suite
    enforces that rather than leaving it to whoever remembers - it fails the build if a project
    carrying the switch contains a [Fact] or a [Theory], and equally if a project with no cases
    omits it.
  -->

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.v3" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Playwright" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Yello.Contracts, Yello.Tests.Shared and Yello.Infrastructure - and still NOT Yello.Client.

    It is here so the boot check computes its expected `<html lang>` from
    CultureSelection.Resolve against SupportedCultures - the same single source the client and
    the Host both read - rather than hard-coding "en". Today every locale resolves to "en"
    because there is one neutral resource set, so a literal would pass and would keep passing
    for the wrong reason: the day a `de` translation lands, `de-AT` must start resolving to
    `de`, and a test carrying a literal would then assert the old answer.

    No reference to Yello.Client. This suite drives the client as a BLACK BOX over HTTP, which
    is the whole point of a browser level - a compile-time edge to the thing under test would
    let a case assert against a type instead of against what the browser actually received.
    The dev server is located by build-output convention instead; see ClientDevServer.

    Story 1.9 (AC11) added the other two for RealHostCollection: the real client in Chromium
    against a real Host on a real database. Yello.Tests.Shared carries the Host harness
    (RunningHost, ProductActor) and the one SQL Server engine; Yello.Infrastructure is there for
    the schema only - the collection's database is created by running the migrations through
    YelloDbContext, as the isolation and revocation suites do, because AR-36 forbids the Host
    applying them. Neither is the thing under test, so the black-box rule above still holds.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Yello.Contracts\Yello.Contracts.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Yello.Tests.Shared\Yello.Tests.Shared.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Yello.Infrastructure\Yello.Infrastructure.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
